speechtext:
In connection with clause 122 I move, Mr. Chairman:
That the said act be further amended by adding thereto, immediately after section 4 thereof, the following section:
"4A. Notwithstanding anything in this act, no pension may be paid to any person for or commencing in
(a)	any month before January, 1966 in which that person had not attained 70 years of age;
(b)	any month before January, 1967 in which that person had not attained 69 years of age;
(c)	any month before January, 1968 in which that person had not attained 68 years of age;
(d)	any month before January, 1969 in which that person had not atained 67 years of age; or

Canada Pension Plan
(e)	any month before January, 1970 in which that person had not atained 66 years of age".
This clause is adapted from clause 121 of part IV, which in that part dealt with the age-adjusted pension. The effect of this clause is to provide for the progressive reduction of the age of eligibility from 70 years to 65 years by 1970. For example, a person who in January, 1966, has attained the age of 69 years will be eligible for the full amount of the monthly pension, and so on successively to the year 1970 when all persons who have attained age 65 will become eligible.

My question of privilege is in regard to clause 124 of part IV of the bill that we are considering. I relate this to the remarks I made previous to my question. I do not wish to read all this letter, but I want to put some points in this letter on the record in connection with the section of the act that we are now considering. It is an extremely formal letter, written by a Mr. Don Russell, with whom I must say I am not extremely well acquainted; but he says;
Dear Sir;
I am-

Thank you, Mr. Chairman. If Your Honour will watch the hon. member, who is not in his seat, at the other end of the chamber, I will be pleased to continue. The letter says:
Dear Sir;
I am sending you this letter as a farmer and a member of your constituency, in the hope that I may impress upon you the urgent need for something substantial being applied to the government's agricultural policy.
